                  </origin>
                  <provenance type="#l"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_537">Syon, Middlesex,
                        Bridgettine abbey of St Saviour, St Mary the Virgin, and St
                        Bridget</orgName><p>Graded Bridgettine calendar. Added obits in one hand
                        include <q>Dame Constans browne ye VIth abbas</q> at 16 July (d. 1530),
                        Thomas Fishburn, <q>first confessor of this monastery</q> (13 September),
                           <q>Obit of our founder</q> (30 August). Bridget follows Anne in the
                        litany as 2nd of virgins. </p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners"><q><persName role="fmo">Edmund
                           Gostlings</persName> book Witham Place 1740</q>, f.i.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Bookplate of <persName role="fmo">Lord
                        Stourton</persName> (? 19th Lord S., d. 1872).</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Sold at Sotheby's 11 July 1966, lot 223
                     (Property of the late <persName role="fmo">Lord Mowbray, Segrave, and
                        Stourton</persName>).</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Dawsons catalogue 218 (1970), item 11
                     (£3750). This was the last known location when the book was first reported in
                     the supplement to Medieval Libraries of Great Britain (1987).</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Believed to have been sold at the London
                     antiquarian book fair in 1971 or 1972, possibly to Japan (letter 21 July
                     1975).</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">The Schoenberg Database of Manuscripts <ref
                        target="https://sdbm.library.upenn.edu/manuscripts/4361">SDBM_MS_4361</ref>
                     records that this book appeared Sam Fogg Catalogue 14 (1991) and Tenschert
                     catalogue 41 (1993). </provenance>
               </history>
